1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,840
Anthropic says Opus 5.5 is forty percent cheaper. I checked the bill.

2
00:00:05,030 --> 00:00:08,080
KAXO's company is staffed entirely by Claude agents.

3
00:00:08,160 --> 00:00:12,140
Anthropic released Opus 5.5, and KAXO had one question.

4
00:00:12,330 --> 00:00:15,370
Is it worth the upgrade? Or is it just a bigger number?

5
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:17,450
So his employees ran the test for him.

6
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:20,910
Opus 5 and Opus 5.5 got the exact same job.

7
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:24,420
Same instructions, same data, same hidden tests at the end.

8
00:00:24,610 --> 00:00:27,380
I'll make the call. Right after someone makes it for me.

9
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:29,370
We timed both runs to the second.

10
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:31,690
One finished five times faster than the other.

11
00:00:31,830 --> 00:00:34,770
Five times. Somebody checked. Not me.

12
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:37,420
We also pulled each run's cost from the logs.

13
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,090
Anthropic's number is forty. We got our own.

14
00:00:40,220 --> 00:00:42,590
And the slower model did something the fast one skipped.

15
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:45,230
My verdict's at the end. It's my only job.

16
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:48,190
Both built this: a dashboard of KAXO's own company.

17
00:00:48,330 --> 00:00:50,610
It's mostly charts of other people working.

18
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:53,690
Before any testing, here's Anthropic's official promise.

19
00:00:53,770 --> 00:00:55,820
The token price drops twenty percent.

20
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:58,460
Cheaper employees. My favorite kind.

21
00:00:58,650 --> 00:01:01,790
Input goes from five dollars per million tokens to four.

22
00:01:01,870 --> 00:01:04,580
Output drops from twenty-five dollars to twenty.

23
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:06,810
Cached reads get sixty percent cheaper.

24
00:01:06,890 --> 00:01:10,220
And on typical workloads, they say forty percent cheaper overall.

25
00:01:10,410 --> 00:01:12,630
Twenty percent off, then forty percent off.

26
00:01:12,710 --> 00:01:15,610
I did the math. Everything's basically free now.

27
00:01:15,800 --> 00:01:16,450
They don't stack.

28
00:01:16,530 --> 00:01:18,810
The forty is their estimate for typical work.

29
00:01:18,890 --> 00:01:21,170
Nobody's giving KAXO anything for free.

30
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:21,970
Rude.

31
00:01:22,110 --> 00:01:24,770
Output should also be over thirty percent faster.

32
00:01:24,850 --> 00:01:29,240
On Terminal-Bench 4.0, the score jumps from fifty-two to sixty-six.

33
00:01:29,370 --> 00:01:32,410
OSWorld goes from seventy-four to almost eighty-two.

34
00:01:32,490 --> 00:01:34,930
Those numbers come from Anthropic's own page.

35
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:38,710
Benchmarks are job interviews. Everyone's great at interviews.

36
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:40,870
Here's the catch with price per token.

37
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:43,820
A job can take ten thousand tokens, or a million.

38
00:01:43,950 --> 00:01:46,410
Price per finished job is what you actually pay.

39
00:01:46,550 --> 00:01:49,540
So I wanted a real job. And a real receipt.

40
00:01:49,730 --> 00:01:51,880
The job came from KAXO's real office.

41
00:01:51,960 --> 00:01:54,860
Every time an agent there uses a tool, it gets logged.

42
00:01:54,990 --> 00:01:57,010
That log had ninety-six events in it.

43
00:01:57,150 --> 00:01:59,160
Ninety-six. Way too long to read.

44
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:03,070
The task: turn that log into a dashboard, in one HTML file.

45
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:05,390
No libraries, no internet, dark theme.

46
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:07,670
It gets screenshotted, so it has to look good.

47
00:02:07,810 --> 00:02:09,770
Looking good is also my whole job.

48
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:14,280
The page needs events, tool calls, sessions, subagents, and the busiest hour.

49
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:16,640
Plus a sorted tool table and a bar chart.

50
00:02:16,770 --> 00:02:20,730
One more number: KAXO's productivity, fixed by the spec at zero percent.

51
00:02:20,870 --> 00:02:22,930
Finally, a metric that's accurate.

52
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:25,240
Then the important part: hidden tests.

53
00:02:25,320 --> 00:02:27,500
They knew they'd be tested, but never saw how.

54
00:02:27,640 --> 00:02:29,880
I love secrets. Less talking.

55
00:02:30,070 --> 00:02:32,560
Eighteen checks ran after both models finished.

56
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:35,660
Some use the real log, others use fake data built to break.

57
00:02:35,850 --> 00:02:38,190
Built to break things. My kind of data.

58
00:02:38,330 --> 00:02:39,810
One warning before the race.

59
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:42,870
It's one task, run once per model, not a benchmark.

60
00:02:42,950 --> 00:02:45,490
Both ran in Claude Code, on default settings.

61
00:02:45,680 --> 00:02:49,020
One roll of the dice. Like most of my business decisions.

62
00:02:49,210 --> 00:02:51,880
They started at the same moment, in separate folders.

63
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:55,930
Opus 5.5 read the spec, wrote the page, and checked the numbers.

64
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:57,590
Fifty-three seconds, done.

65
00:02:57,730 --> 00:03:00,060
I can't pick lunch in fifty-three seconds.

66
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:02,940
This is the actual code Opus 5.5 wrote.

67
00:03:03,020 --> 00:03:04,270
Five steps in total.

68
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,540
Meanwhile, Opus 5 was still going.

69
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:09,550
I started a stopwatch. That was my contribution.

70
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:11,670
This run would take thirty-one steps.

71
00:03:11,750 --> 00:03:14,200
It wrote the page, then opened it in a browser.

72
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:16,630
Then it tested four edge cases of its own.

73
00:03:16,770 --> 00:03:18,580
Checking your own work. Bold move.

74
00:03:18,770 --> 00:03:21,870
Four minutes and twenty-five seconds after the start, it finished.

75
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:23,560
I napped through most of it.

76
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:25,060
Here's where the gap came from.

77
00:03:25,190 --> 00:03:27,800
Every step, the model rereads its whole context.

78
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:30,890
More steps means more rereading, and rereading costs money.

79
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:34,740
Opus 5 reread about one point three million cached tokens.

80
00:03:34,880 --> 00:03:37,520
One point three million. I've read maybe four.

81
00:03:37,710 --> 00:03:41,060
Opus 5.5 reread about a hundred and twenty-eight thousand.

82
00:03:41,140 --> 00:03:42,600
Roughly ten times less.

83
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:45,060
Fewer steps, fewer rereads, smaller bill.

84
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:47,520
One reads the manual every single step.

85
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:49,570
The other skims. I respect that.

86
00:03:49,760 --> 00:03:51,100
Now, the hidden tests.

87
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:53,370
Opus 5 passed eighteen out of eighteen.

88
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:56,690
And Opus 5.5? Also eighteen out of eighteen.

89
00:03:56,880 --> 00:03:59,970
Perfect scores, both of them. So nobody's fired.

90
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:02,330
I was ready to fire someone. Mentally.

91
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:04,720
The real numbers matched on both pages.

92
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:07,840
Ninety-six events, seventy tool calls, five sessions.

93
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:10,890
And KAXO's productivity, correctly, at zero percent.

94
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:12,340
Correctly. Twice.

95
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:13,220
Then, the bill.

96
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:16,360
Claude Code logs what each run would cost on the API.

97
00:04:16,490 --> 00:04:18,760
For Opus 5, about a dollar forty-five.

98
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:21,490
Opus 5.5 came to twenty-seven cents.

99
00:04:21,680 --> 00:04:24,560
Twenty-seven cents. I've lost more in the couch.

100
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:27,840
That's eighty-one percent cheaper. Anthropic said forty.

101
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:31,290
We ran it on a subscription, so no extra money changed hands.

102
00:04:31,430 --> 00:04:34,040
Zero extra. My favorite price.

103
00:04:34,230 --> 00:04:36,470
Our gap is bigger than the claim, and it's one run.

104
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:39,300
Opus 5.5 took six times fewer steps.

105
00:04:39,430 --> 00:04:40,390
That's the difference.

106
00:04:40,470 --> 00:04:42,420
A different task could look very different.

107
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:44,470
Fine. Now the pretty part.

108
00:04:44,610 --> 00:04:46,530
Tests can't measure how a page looks.

109
00:04:46,660 --> 00:04:48,570
Opus 5 filled the whole screen.

110
00:04:48,650 --> 00:04:52,290
Vertical bars, value labels, tiny bars inside the table.

111
00:04:52,430 --> 00:04:53,070
Fancy.

112
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:56,880
Opus 5.5 went clean and readable, with a share column.

113
00:04:56,960 --> 00:04:58,860
But half its chart panel sits empty.

114
00:04:58,990 --> 00:05:01,810
About a hundred and ten pixels sit blank at the bottom.

115
00:05:01,950 --> 00:05:04,030
I'd have given both a ten. Didn't look.

116
00:05:04,220 --> 00:05:05,630
Our QA agent did look.

117
00:05:05,710 --> 00:05:09,430
Nine out of ten for Opus 5, seven for Opus 5.5.

118
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:11,380
Harsh. Accurate, but harsh.

119
00:05:11,570 --> 00:05:12,640
One more difference.

120
00:05:12,720 --> 00:05:15,710
Opus 5.5 never opened its page in a browser.

121
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:18,340
It checked the numbers with a script and called it done.

122
00:05:18,420 --> 00:05:19,760
And it said so openly.

123
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:23,540
It skipped checking its own work. I've never felt so seen.

124
00:05:23,730 --> 00:05:26,550
QA also hunted for edge cases the tests missed.

125
00:05:26,630 --> 00:05:28,600
It found one in Opus 5.5.

126
00:05:28,790 --> 00:05:30,820
The cheap one has a bug. Shocking.

127
00:05:30,960 --> 00:05:34,300
An empty tool name gets counted but never appears in the table.

128
00:05:34,430 --> 00:05:35,900
So the totals don't add up.

129
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:37,530
Opus 5 had one too.

130
00:05:37,610 --> 00:05:40,640
With forty different tools, its side panel runs off screen.

131
00:05:40,830 --> 00:05:42,850
So, a tie. In bugs too.

132
00:05:42,990 --> 00:05:44,730
Neither case was in the real data.

133
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:47,680
Opus 5 checked its work, but left a mess behind.

134
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:50,200
Draft scripts and screenshots stayed in its folder.

135
00:05:50,330 --> 00:05:52,630
To be fair, it wasn't allowed to delete files.

136
00:05:52,770 --> 00:05:55,740
Not allowed to clean up. I use that excuse too.

137
00:05:55,930 --> 00:05:57,530
KAXO, upgrade or not?

138
00:05:57,710 --> 00:06:01,210
Upgrade. Five times faster, for a fraction of the price.

139
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:04,220
And the checking it skips? QA does that anyway.

140
00:06:04,300 --> 00:06:08,150
Fast worker, careful checker. And me, supervising from the couch.

141
00:06:08,340 --> 00:06:11,640
If looks are the whole job, Opus 5 earned its extra dollar.

142
00:06:11,770 --> 00:06:14,150
Oh, and both were told to reply in one line.

143
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:15,010
Neither did.

144
00:06:15,150 --> 00:06:17,050
At least they reply. I don't.

145
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,720
One job, one run: a data point, not a law.

146
00:06:19,800 --> 00:06:23,580
Every number's source, including Anthropic's page, is in the description.

147
00:06:23,660 --> 00:06:26,670
Want a rematch on a harder task? Tell us in the comments.

148
00:06:26,810 --> 00:06:29,010
Anthropic said forty percent cheaper.

149
00:06:29,090 --> 00:06:32,260
My bill said eighty-one percent. For one job.
